    // ── Judgement graph (judgement-graph resolver slice, PR1) ──────────────
    //
    // PLANE CONTRACT for every plain method in this section (review round 1,
    // finding 4 — same contract `merge_nodes`/`find_node_id_by_identifier`
    // already have, made explicit here rather than left implicit): a plain
    // method opens its OWN transaction directly against `self.pool` with NO
    // `app.account_id` scoping, so it is only safe to call against a pool
    // that either (a) is the ORG TENANT plane (`workers::embed::JobScope::Org`
    // — this table's tenant-tree RLS is a DIFFERENT, currently-inert policy
    // keyed on `app.user_id`/`tenant_rls_visible_graph_ids()`, not
    // `app.account_id`), or (b) connects as a role that bypasses the
    // consumer-plane `account_isolation` policy (true of every pool in this
    // repo's tests today; NOT true of production once OPS-4's `coralswarm_app`
    // is live on a given call path). On the CONSUMER plane, callers reachable
    // from `JobScope::Consumer` MUST use the `_scoped` sibling inside a
    // `tenancy::scope::begin_account_scoped` transaction instead. Misuse (a plain call against an
    // enforcing consumer-plane pool) fails LOUD: Postgres raises
    // `unrecognized configuration parameter "app.account_id"` (SQLSTATE
    // `42704`) the moment the policy's `current_setting(...)` predicate
    // evaluates with nothing set — a hard error every time, never a silently
    // empty/wrong result — and [`PostgresStore`]'s override of each plain
    // method below rewrites that specific error into an actionable
    // [`StoreError::Db`] message naming the method and the fix, rather than
    // leaving a bare SQLSTATE for the caller to puzzle out.

    /// Write one judgement (design doc §5.1's concurrency-safe flow, review
    /// round 1 findings 1/2 tightened, round 2 CodeRabbit Major "endpoint
    /// validation" added as step 1.5):
    ///
    /// 1. `pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtextextended(graph_id||':'||a||':'||b, 0))`,
    ///    keyed on the CANONICAL (ordered) pair — the transaction's first
    ///    statement, auto-released at commit/rollback.
    /// 1.5. `SELECT count(*) FROM nodes WHERE node_id = ANY(pair) AND graph_id
    ///    = new.graph_id` must be exactly 2, or [`StoreError::JudgementEndpointMissing`]
    ///    and nothing written. `node_a_id`/`node_b_id` carry no FK (migration
    ///    header), so this is the code-level stand-in: it catches both a
    ///    cross-graph pair (which would corrupt the `graph_id`-anchored RLS
    ///    policy's meaning) and a pair naming an already-deleted node.
    ///    Applied to every `source`, including `backfill` — PR5's backfill
    ///    script (design doc §7.1/§7.2) already existence-gates every row it
    ///    writes before calling this, so a conforming backfill caller always
    ///    satisfies this trivially; checking unconditionally rather than
    ///    exempting `source=backfill` closes the gap if a future backfill
    ///    implementation ever forgets its own gate, at zero cost to the
    ///    documented-conforming case. **Forward note for PR2:** this proves
    ///    liveness only at THIS judgement write's own time — PR2's
    ///    `merge_nodes_with_judgement`/the `node_merges.judgement_id`
    ///    back-link population must independently confirm the merge's
    ///    `(keep_id, dup_id)` pair still matches this judgement's `graph_id`
    ///    + canonical pair before writing that link, since time passes
    ///    between a judgement being written and a merge materializing it.
    /// 2. `SELECT ... WHERE annulled_at IS NULL FOR UPDATE` (deliberately NOT
    ///    expiry-filtered — see step 3's first bullet), now serialized
    ///    against every other writer for this exact pair.
    /// 3. Branch on what's found:
    ///    - active row is EXPIRED (`expires_at IS NOT NULL AND expires_at <=
    ///      now()` — only possible for an UNSURE row) → annul it
    ///      unconditionally (`annulled_by_kind = system`, `annul_reason =
    ///      'expired'`) and fall through to insert-and-supersede below.
    ///      "Live" = `annulled_at IS NULL AND (expires_at IS NULL OR
    ///      expires_at > now())`; an expired-but-unannulled row is never live,
    ///      so it NEVER blocks via `HumanJudgementExists` (even if
    ///      `source='human'`) and never matches as a same-outcome no-op — the
    ///      partial unique index only keys on `annulled_at`, so this row MUST
    ///      be annulled before the insert below or it would 23505.
    ///    - (row is live) active `source='human'` row, `new.source != Human`
    ///      → [`StoreError::HumanJudgementExists`], nothing written.
    ///    - (row is live) a TRUE idempotent replay — same verdict, same
    ///      `source`, AND same `prompt_version`/`model_id`/`confidence`/
    ///      `evidence`/`created_by` — → race-resolution no-op,
    ///      `Ok(existing_id)`, no duplicate insert. This is deliberately
    ///      NARROW (review round 1, finding 1): two concurrent writers
    ///      producing the IDENTICAL row resolve here, but a re-adjudication
    ///      that reuses the same verdict/source with FRESH metadata (a
    ///      prompt-version bump's new evidence, a later confidence number)
    ///      does not — it falls to the next bullet so the new metadata is
    ///      actually recorded, not silently dropped.
    ///    - (row is live) anything else — same verdict/source with different
    ///      metadata (reason `"re-adjudication"`), or a genuinely different
    ///      verdict/source (reason names the new verdict/source) — annul it
    ///      (`annulled_at`/`annulled_by`/`annulled_by_kind`/`annul_reason`)
    ///      and fall through to insert-and-supersede.
    ///    - no active row → insert with nothing to supersede.
    /// 4. `INSERT ... RETURNING judgement_id`.
    /// 5. If step 3 annulled a row, stamp its `superseded_by` with the new id.
    ///
    /// NOT an `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE` — see design doc §5.1 for
    /// why (a partial unique INDEX can't be `DEFERRABLE`, and `DO UPDATE`
    /// would mutate the row in place rather than preserving it as history).
    /// Cloud-only; default body errors, [`PostgresStore`] overrides it. See
    /// this section's own PLANE CONTRACT note above.
    async fn write_judgement(&self, _new: NewJudgement) -> Result<Uuid, StoreError> {
        Err(StoreError::Db(Box::from(
            "write_judgement: not implemented for this Store backend",
        )))
    }

    /// Annul `judgement_id`: stamp `annulled_at`/`annulled_by`/
    /// `annulled_by_kind`/`annul_reason`. Idempotent — annulling an
    /// already-annulled (or unknown) `judgement_id` is a clean
    /// `Ok(AnnulOutcome::AlreadyAnnulled { .. })`, same no-op convention as
    /// [`Store::unmerge_nodes`], just no longer a bare `()` (review round 1,
    /// finding 3 — a caller like PR3's undo hook needs to know which
    /// happened, and what superseded the row in the already-annulled case).
    ///
    /// **Takes the SAME pair advisory lock [`Store::write_judgement`] does**
    /// (review round 1, finding 3 — the original cut had no lock at all,
    /// which let an annul and a concurrent `write_judgement` for the
    /// identical pair interleave with no serialization between the two code
    /// paths, only within each). Implementation: read the row BY ID first
    /// (in whatever state) to recover `(graph_id, node_a_id, node_b_id)` —
    /// the id alone doesn't name the pair, so the lock key can't be built
    /// without this lookup — then take the lock, then re-check the row's
    /// CURRENT `annulled_at` under it (the unlocked read can be stale by the
    /// time the lock is acquired), then annul. Cloud-only; default body
    /// errors, [`PostgresStore`] overrides it. See this section's own PLANE
    /// CONTRACT note above.
