    // ── RLS-scoped transaction surface: atoms/nodes/graphs cluster ─────────────
    // (org-tenancy rollout, PR B.0 chunk 1 — see `tenancy::scope::begin_account_scoped`
    // and migration `0083_consumer_rls`.)
    //
    // Sibling `_scoped` variants of the `atoms`/`nodes`/`graphs` methods above,
    // each taking an externally-supplied `&mut sqlx::Transaction<'_,
    // sqlx::Postgres>` in place of reaching into `&self.pool` directly. This is
    // the trait-surface half of wiring `begin_account_scoped` into real call
    // sites: a future route handler opens ONE scoped transaction — which sets
    // the `app.account_id` session GUC once — via `Tenant::begin_account_scoped`,
    // then threads that same `&mut tx` through however many `_scoped` calls it
    // needs. Every query in every call runs against that one already-GUC'd
    // connection, so each content table's RLS policy (migration
    // `0083_consumer_rls`) sees the right `app.account_id` — whether the
    // table has its own direct `account_id` column (`graphs`) or is scoped via
    // a `graph_id IN (SELECT id FROM graphs WHERE account_id = ...)` subquery
    // (`atoms`, `nodes`, and the one-hop siblings some of these operations
    // inherently also touch in the same statement/transaction, e.g.
    // `node_atoms`).
    //
    // Each method's SQL is byte-identical to its unscoped twin above; the only
    // differences are (1) the transaction comes from the caller instead of
    // `self.pool.begin()`, and (2) nothing here calls `tx.commit()` /
    // `tx.rollback()` — transaction lifecycle belongs to whoever opened it,
    // since one transaction is shared across multiple `_scoped` calls. Where
    // the unscoped twin used a local "nothing to do, commit early, return" or
    // "validation failed as a plain value check (not a driver error), roll
    // back this method's own private transaction, return" shortcut, the
    // `_scoped` twin just returns without touching the transaction — safe
    // because no write happened yet at that point, so the shared transaction
    // is left exactly as the caller's prior calls left it, ready for the
    // caller's next `_scoped` call or its own final commit. (A REAL driver/SQL
    // error from any bound query still propagates via `?` as normal, which —
    // same as any Postgres transaction — leaves the whole shared transaction
    // aborted until the caller rolls it back; that's inherent to sharing one
    // transaction across multiple calls, not something any `_scoped` method
    // can locally paper over.)
    //
    // A default body (a `StoreError::Db` "not implemented" error) is provided
    // here; `PostgresStore` overrides every one of these below.
    //
    // **Nothing called any of these when this block landed** — route/worker
    // conversion was later, incremental work per method-owning-route, gated
    // on OPS-4 actually running. Both have since happened: the conversions
    // shipped (org-tenancy phases B.x — `scripts/rls_coverage.manifest` is
    // the per-file ledger) and OPS-4 has run, so these `_scoped` methods are
    // the live, enforcing path.
    //
    // NOT every atoms/nodes/graphs-touching method got a `_scoped` twin in
    // this first chunk. Left out, each for a reason too substantial to reduce
    // to a mechanical "swap `self.pool` for the caller's `tx`" transform —
    // see this PR's description for the full reasoning:
    //   - `create_team_tx` — despite one `INSERT INTO graphs`, it's
    //     fundamentally a `teams`/`team_keys`/`team_members` operation, out of
    //     this chunk's 3-table scope; belongs in a later "teams" chunk.
    //   - `merge_nodes` / `unmerge_nodes` — large, atomically touch several
    //     OTHER RLS-covered tables (`node_merges`, `node_atoms`,
    //     `relationship_edge`, `supersession_edge`, `node_promotions`), and
    //     contain application-level conditional `tx.rollback()` branches that
    //     assume they own a private transaction.
    //   - `promote_node` — spans TWO separately-committed sub-transactions
    //     (an early mid-method `tx.commit()` followed by further,
    //     non-transactional work that itself calls `self.copy_atoms_to_graph()`
    //     and `self.link_node_atom()`); can't be shared-transaction-ified
    //     without redesigning its commit boundaries.
    //   - `link_node_atom` — swallows a driver-level FK-constraint violation
    //     via `tx.rollback()` + `Ok(())`; under a caller-owned shared
    //     transaction that soft-skip needs a Postgres SAVEPOINT (the same
    //     technique `batch_apply_remote_ops` already uses at review H11f for
    //     the identical class of problem) to work at all — real design work,
    //     not a mechanical copy.
    //   - `ocean_stats` — runs 5 aggregate queries via `tokio::try_join!` plus
    //     N per-dimension-breakdown queries via `futures::future::join_all`,
    //     genuinely concurrently against the pool (a deliberate, documented
    //     latency optimization for a page-load-blocking endpoint). A `tx: &mut
    //     Transaction` can't be borrowed concurrently by multiple in-flight
    //     queries, so a faithful `_scoped` twin would have to serialize all of
    //     them — a real perf regression for a hot read path, and arguably the
    //     wrong tool for a pure-read fan-out in the first place.

    /// Scoped sibling of [`Store::list_graphs`].
    async fn list_graphs_scoped(
        &self,
        _tx: &mut sqlx::Transaction<'_, sqlx::Postgres>,
        _account_id: Uuid,
    ) -> Result<Vec<Graph>, StoreError> {
        Err(StoreError::Db(Box::from(
            "list_graphs_scoped: not implemented for this Store backend",
        )))
    }

