    // ── RLS-scoped transaction surface: the final seven (+ four found along
    // the way) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // (org-tenancy rollout, last known OPS-4 Store-surface gap.) Seven
    // methods had no `_scoped` twin at all: `vector_search`, `keyword_search`,
    // `atom_content_refs` (the ask/search retrieval core —
    // `multi_graph_retrieve`/`fetch_atom_content`/`citation_details` in
    // routes/queries.rs and services/ask.rs issue exactly these),
    // `list_session_atoms`/`count_session_atoms` (GET /v1/sessions/{id} —
    // previously a hard 500 with no scoped path at all), and
    // `relabel_summary_owner`/`list_shared_session_corals` (previously
    // best-effort warn-and-degrade arms). While wiring `citation_details`'s
    // atom-metadata enrichment it became clear four more methods it (and
    // several already-"converted" route files) call needed twins too —
    // `atom_inline_content`, `atom_authors`, `atom_channels`,
    // `atom_provenance` — all thin wrappers over the same `atoms` table (the
    // latter three share the private `atom_field_pair`/`atom_field_pair_scoped`
    // helper below `impl PostgresStore`). Leaving those four unscoped while
    // "converting" citation_details would have moved the gap one call deeper
    // instead of closing it.
    //
    // Same rules as every other chunk: caller-supplied `&mut
    // sqlx::Transaction<'_, sqlx::Postgres>`, no local commit/rollback,
    // default `Err` body here, SQL byte-identical to the
    // unscoped twin. `vector_search`/`keyword_search` were checked for a
    // pgvector/RLS query-plan interaction (HNSW index use) before shipping —
    // see this PR's description for the finding.

